  "query_preview": "/WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nIssued at 214 PM CDT Mon Sep 7 2026\n\nCold front isn't expected to be out of our area until early \nafternoon on Wednesday, which will keep precipitation chances over \nmuch of the area through the first half of the day. We will see \nshower/storm coverage decrease from northwest to southeast through \nthe morning, with best chances for precipitation in the afternoon \nhours for areas in western Illinois and northeastern Missouri. At \nthis time, we are not expecting much in the way of strong-severe \nstorms. Much of the remaining instability will have been worked \nthrough, leaving us with a shield of stratiform rain and little/no \nembedded thunder. Otherwise, we will see mostly cloudy conditions \nthrough the day, with temperatures in the low 80s and light west to \nnorthwest winds. \n\nThursday into Friday, upper level flow becomes more northwesterly as \na weak wave passes through and weak ridging builds over the Central \nCONUS. This wave will be through our area by midday Thursday, with \nsurface high pressure moving in behind. A brief period of quiet and \nseasonal weather will result, with mostly clear skies and \ntemperatures around 80 on Thursday into Friday. Overnight \ntemperatures will cool off a bit compared to recent nights, with \nmost in the mid 50s. Friday, the weak ridge will pass east of the \narea, with southwesterly flow increasing ahead of a quickly-\napproaching shortwave trof. This wave is due to come through Friday \nnight into Saturday, prompting our next chance of precipitation (30-\n60% chances). Showers and storms will be possible along the \nassociated cold front, but accumulations should be on the lower-end \ndue to the quick movement of this wave. There remains some \nuncertainty, owing to moisture availability. \n\nSaturday and beyond appear to be a continuation of potentially \nactive weather, with us falling largely under upper level zonal to \nsouthwesterly flow. This patten would favor us falling under the \npath of passing shortwaves through the weekend and into next week. \nThus, daily PoPs (20-40%+) are in the forecast through this stretch, \nwith temperatures remaining above seasonal norms.",
